<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<subsection xml:id="ai_policy" label="ai_policy">
  <title>Use of Artificial Intelligence</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Generative artificial intelligence (<init>AI</init>) tools (<eg/> ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot) can be a useful study aid, but they cannot do the work of learning for you.
      The goal of this course is to develop <em>your</em> understanding of the material, and that understanding is built only by working through problems yourself.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <subsubsection xml:id="ai_quizzes_exams" label="ai_quizzes_exams">
    <title>Quizzes and Exams</title>
    <p>
      The use of <init>AI</init> tools, or of any electronic device that can access them, is <alert>prohibited</alert> during all quizzes and exams.
      Any such use will be treated as a case of academic dishonesty under the <xref ref="academic_honor_code" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <subsubsection xml:id="ai_homework_studying" label="ai_homework_studying">
    <title>Homework and Studying</title>
    <p>
      The same standard applies to <init>AI</init> that applies to working with classmates, in the sense that whatever help you use, you must be able to reproduce every solution on your own.
      Within that standard, <init>AI</init> tools are permitted.
      Productive uses include
      <ul>
        <li>asking for a different explanation of a definition or concept,</li>
        <li>generating extra practice problems,</li>
        <li>asking it to check work you have already completed.</li>
      </ul>
      However, asking it to solve an assigned problem before you have made a serious attempt yourself is generally <alert>not productive</alert>, since it removes the practice that the assignment is meant to provide.
    </p>

    <warning xml:id="ai_warning">
      <p>
        <init>AI</init> tools frequently produce answers that are confidently wrong.
        Until you understand the material well enough to check their work, you will not be able to tell whether the tool has helped you or misled you.
        Understanding the material yourself is therefore a prerequisite for using these tools effectively, not something they can replace.
      </p>
    </warning>
  </subsubsection>
</subsection>
